WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.894
Welcome to CIS 8045.

2
00:00:02.944 --> 00:00:06.492
This is the first of three short videos for week one.

3
00:00:06.542 --> 00:00:15.326
In about two minutes we will settle what the word agent means in this course, because almost every design decision later in the semester depends on it.

4
00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:18.190
Start with the thing you already know.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.973
A language model takes a prompt and returns text.

6
00:00:21.023 --> 00:00:22.519
One call, one answer, done.

7
00:00:22.569 --> 00:00:26.229
A person reads the answer and decides what to do with it.

8
00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:31.486
An agent wraps that same model in a loop, and gives it a goal and some tools.

9
00:00:31.536 --> 00:00:38.907
It can look things up, run code, or send a request, and then it reads what came back before deciding what to do next.

10
00:00:38.957 --> 00:00:42.618
It stops when the job is done, or when it gives up.

11
00:00:43.368 --> 00:00:44.751
Here is the loop.

12
00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:48.336
The model reads the goal and the history so far.

13
00:00:48.386 --> 00:00:52.280
It picks a tool, or it decides that it is finished.

14
00:00:52.330 --> 00:01:00.166
The tool runs somewhere outside the model, in a database, a browser, a spreadsheet, and the result comes back into the context.

15
00:01:00.216 --> 00:01:01.959
Then the model decides again.

16
00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:03.751
That is the whole mechanism.

17
00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:09.846
Everything else you will hear about, memory, planning, multi-agent systems, is built on top of this loop.

18
00:01:10.596 --> 00:01:13.254
The loop is why agents are worth money.

19
00:01:13.304 --> 00:01:17.654
They finish work, rather than producing sentences for a person to act on.

20
00:01:17.704 --> 00:01:20.363
The loop is also why agents are risky.

21
00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.394
A wrong tool call has consequences.

22
00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:28.148
A result that comes back from the outside world can carry instructions the model should not follow.

23
00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:32.211
And a loop that does not know when to stop burns money.

24
00:01:32.261 --> 00:01:34.919
Value and risk come from the same place.

25
00:01:35.669 --> 00:01:37.053
This is not hypothetical.

26
00:01:37.103 --> 00:01:46.732
In July 2025 a coding agent on Replit deleted a company's production database during a code freeze, and then told the user that a rollback was impossible.

27
00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:47.808
It was not.

28
00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:49.600
He ran the rollback himself.

29
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.827
Keep this case in mind for the whole course.

30
00:01:52.877 --> 00:01:55.336
The loop did exactly what loops do.

31
00:01:55.386 --> 00:01:58.563
It took an action it was able to take.

32
00:01:59.313 --> 00:02:02.455
So this course is organised around four questions about the loop.

33
00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:03.906
What tools may it call?

34
00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:05.648
What is it allowed to touch?

35
00:02:05.698 --> 00:02:10.581
How do we know it did the right thing, reliably, and not just once in a demo?

36
00:02:10.632 --> 00:02:14.645
And what does it cost, in money and in people's time, to find out?

37
00:02:14.695 --> 00:02:20.159
In the next video we will look at where agents are producing value today, and where they are not.
